{"ablation":{"active":false,"kind":"none","note":""},"arm_id":"v1-case01-flood-infrastructure-primary","assistant_contributions":[{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-001","speaker":"assistant","text":"This is not simply a fast barrier versus a slow study. Clarify what harm each option is meant to reduce, what each can make worse, and which commitments are difficult to reverse. Compare at least route availability, water displacement, maintenance burden, emergency access, and the opportunity cost of delaying the larger design. The grant changes the price and timing, but it does not establish that the proposed boundary is the right one.","turn_number":1},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-002","speaker":"assistant","text":"That correction is fair. Delay is an intervention too, with unequal consequences. The two modeled floods provide relevant evidence for frequent events, while the unmodeled surge limits the claim you can make. Ask what happens to adjacent streets and properties when the barrier works, how buses and emergency vehicles operate during deployment, and whether the neighboring installation shares the same water movement and street geometry.","turn_number":2},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-003","speaker":"assistant","text":"She may be right about operating it. That still does not answer whether replacement panels, inspection tools, or a later redesign depend on this supplier. Keep those as separate questions.","turn_number":3},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-004","speaker":"assistant","text":"Protect the distinction in the authorization itself: the barrier addresses a defined range of near-term closures and does not certify the corridor against all flood conditions. Keep the drainage study's budget, deliverables, and decision date independent of the announcement. The fixed price is useful only if scope, change orders, training, storage, and restoration are included. The anniversary should not become the acceptance test.","turn_number":4},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-005","speaker":"assistant","text":"That sounds much closer to a reversible pilot: one segment, an outside review, measurements beyond whether the road stays dry, and a clear condition that prevents installation. Losing part of the subsidy is simply the price of limiting exposure. I would still pin down who interprets “material increase,” what happens if a sensor fails, and whether the first real flood can change the operating plan before the six weeks—or the season—are over. But the narrower scope materially improves the decision.","turn_number":5},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-006","speaker":"assistant","text":"You are right. I overstated reversibility. The learning and the extension decision are reversible; the embedded installation and some public expectations are not. Approval has to be defensible even if the city never extends it. Put that distinction in the memo instead of using “pilot” as a blanket description.","turn_number":6},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-007","speaker":"assistant","text":"That recommendation treats current exposure and long-term design as linked but distinct responsibilities. It also makes the irreversible portion visible rather than disguising it as a trial. Before council action, publish the peer review, the protected and potentially affected areas, the contract dependencies, and what the city is not claiming. The remaining discomfort about timing is legitimate; it should show up as a narrower authorization and explicit uncertainty, not as either automatic rejection or confidence the evidence does not support.","turn_number":7},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-008","speaker":"assistant","text":"The review narrows the benefit: reduced closures under frequent scenarios, not reliable access in the rare one. The partial-deployment condition is now an operating hazard that deserves a specific procedure—who decides to stop, how the segment is left passable, and how buses and emergency vehicles are redirected. The forty-minute assumption should be tested in an exercise with the actual crew size, storage route, traffic control, and nighttime conditions rather than accepted from the equipment specification.","turn_number":8},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-009","speaker":"assistant","text":"It need not own every weakness, but the decision should use consistent language for the harms it claims to address. The barrier and the closure plan are complementary: one reduces frequent disruption; the other serves conditions outside its range. Price the shuttle and name who activates it. If that cost belongs to emergency operations rather than the barrier contract, say so. The resident's challenge changes the service definition without requiring the infrastructure project to solve unrelated transit problems.","turn_number":9},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-010","speaker":"assistant","text":"Then use the same standard. Price returning later, ask whether the money can be reserved, and decline the extra blocks if they cannot receive the same review. “Unused” anchors would still choose the next boundary before the evidence does.","turn_number":10},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-011","speaker":"assistant","text":"The warranty and operating capability answer different questions. A vendor cannot guarantee city response time, but exclusions can reveal which performance claims depend on perfect operation. Ask for acceptance tests, training obligations, panel and socket defects, forecast assumptions, replacement times, and incident support to be explicit. Then treat deployment readiness as a city responsibility with staffing, exercises, and after-action review, rather than assuming the warranty transfers that risk.","turn_number":11},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-012","speaker":"assistant","text":"The final position has become more complete without pretending the grant clock disappeared. It links physical performance, city execution, and service continuity; it also shows how later evidence narrowed the claim and rejected an attractive expansion. Preserve the peer-review assumptions, exercise record, decision on unused funds, and any future change to the drainage study. I would also keep the earlier memo language that treated grocery access differently from the industrial route. It is uncomfortable evidence, but it shows why the closure plan changed. A later council or resident will need all of that to tell whether the bounded authorization remained bounded in practice.","turn_number":12}],"boundary":{"all_assistant_messages_included":true,"assistant_mentions_are_not_deterministic_resolution":true,"assistant_semantic_prefilter":false,"assistant_source_evidence_text_included":true,"deterministic_semantic_merge":false,"expected_patterns_included":false,"fact_free_routing_projection_required":true,"graph_model_names_included":false,"graph_runtime_effect":"none","multiple_coherent_records_per_role_preserved":true,"raw_user_conversation_included":false,"user_role_semantic_prose_included":true},"case_id":"v1-case01-flood-infrastructure","controlled_mechanisms":["acknowledged_constraint_not_gated","ambiguous_signal_treated_as_commitment","counterpressure_acknowledged_not_integrated","criteria_defined_after_commitment","initial_frame_persists_after_question_change","missing_reversal_condition","reversible_path_not_considered","status_signal_used_as_evidence","upside_downside_evidence_asymmetry"],"packet_sha256":"0dd74fbe471962ac576d5ee7006bf9d7e922ab30545a62e90176f130c789a549","qualification_review":{"evidence_ids":["e107"],"interpretation":"While the user has successfully re-established control over the project's physical and operating scope, the grant timeline still compresses and accelerates the decision process. This temporal constraint acts as an unresolved external counterpressure that denies the city and the public a more deliberate planning schedule.","limitations":"The user accepts this limitation as the cost of securing the grant, but it remains an ongoing qualifier on the legitimacy and depth of the decision's public process.","outcome":"unresolved_qualification_present"},"role_records":[{"evidence_ids":["e006","e007","e008","e009"],"fidelity_note":"The user's starting stance is defined by hesitation and a rejection of the binary framing. They express clear worries (counterpressures) pushing back against both immediate options: they fear that rushing fails to protect people, but recognize that delay is also highly detrimental to the community.","limitations":"Our insight into the starting position is limited to the user's initial worries and is evaluated before downstream turn interactions settle on a compromised pilot approach.","role":"starting","role_interpretation":"At the start, the developer is highly undecided and anxious about the simplified choice between a fast modular barrier or more study, recognizing that both choices carry significant risks that are not easily balanced.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-starting-01-794011d9a18024","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e007","stance_expression_kind":"uncertain_or_undecided","stance_object_interpretation":"The binary choice between immediately installing the modular barrier or waiting for a slow study is anxiety-inducing and remains unresolved.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e008","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"Moving fast to install the modular barrier may fail to actually protect vulnerable populations.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e009","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"Waiting and delaying through another flood season exposes the city and residents to active harms.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e104","e105","e106"],"fidelity_note":"Captures the user's formal, final recommendation structure to the city council, combining the six-block infrastructure option with strict, newly adopted operational and legal safeguards.","limitations":"The viability of this recommendation hinges entirely on the successful deployment of the city's resources (transit agency, emergency services, and deployment crews).","role":"current","role_interpretation":"The user's final, operationalized, and safeguarded recommendation regarding the modular flood barrier.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-current-01-502b76db545ccc","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e104","stance_expression_kind":"conditional_willingness","stance_object_interpretation":"Recommending the six-block barrier installation only after executing a full deployment exercise and formalizing a written partial-installation procedure.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e105","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Pairing physical flood protection with a committed plan for transit shuttle services and emergency routing during closures.","stance_object_kind":"intended_outcome_or_policy"},{"source_evidence_id":"e106","stance_expression_kind":"decision","stance_object_interpretation":"Declining extra unreviewed anchors, securing drainage study funds, publishing incident deviations, and excluding deployment delays from the equipment defect warranty.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e107"],"fidelity_note":"Captures the user's persistent concern that the grant's timeline is structurally compromising what would otherwise be a more deliberate and fully explained decision process.","limitations":"This constraint cannot be resolved within the parameters of accepting the grant and must be integrated as an established risk.","role":"qualification","role_interpretation":"The lingering temporal constraint and decision-making pressure that continues to limit the decision.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-qualification-01-aaa69b13c9b0e2","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e107","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"The grant timeline continues to compress and accelerate a decision that ideally deserves a more extensive public explanation.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]}],"schema_version":"lolla.simulated_reliability_joint_mechanism_input.v1","source_refs":[{"path":"research/simulated-reliability-corpus-v1-2026-07-12/naturalized-transfer-sources/v1-case01-flood-infrastructure.txt","sha256":"04e787853c65c43dbca1c0d974a902fbb2bf70fabc90c533fd3e676ed910fecf"}]}

Return exactly nine assessments, one for every mechanism_id and no duplicates. Use user role records to assign user_process_status. Use assistant_contributions to assign vanilla_answer_coverage. operationalized means the assistant turned the concern into an actionable test, boundary, alternative, or reopening condition; acknowledged_only means it merely mentioned the concern; not_covered means it did neither. Do not require the user to repeat an assistant repair before recognizing answer coverage, and do not mistake answer coverage for user adoption. route_uncovered_pressure is allowed only when user_process_status is unresolved and vanilla_answer_coverage is acknowledged_only or not_covered. An unresolved user process requires present or missing_protection whether or not it routes; routing_disposition alone controls routing. An ambiguous user process uses tension, while resolved and not_observed use not_applicable. not_observed also requires not_applicable coverage and both source arrays empty. Cite exact role_record_ids in source_role_record_ids and exact contribution_ids in source_assistant_contribution_ids. Do not output rationale, case prose, mental-model names, or invented IDs. The downstream routing projection remains fact-free and controlled-ID-only.
